schemaVersion: "2"
kind: mixin
name: yoki-codex
requires:
  agent: codex
displayName: yoki harness for Codex
description: >-
  Fills the gaps in the sandbox base image and wires ~/.codex to the same
  claude-profiles layers (core + packs + personal) the host uses, so a
  sandboxed Codex session gets the same hooks/rules/agents/skills parity
  yoki-claude already has (task T15 closes the gap yoki-claude's kit left
  open — see yoki-omp's kit for the same shape a generation earlier).

# Rendered by yoki-box at launch, into a temp dir, and never installed as a
# file — same reasoning as the claude kit's spec.yaml.in: {{DOTFILES_ROOT}}
# is a literal path belonging to one checkout, and a sandbox mounts the repo
# at its HOST path, so a generated artifact would be wrong the moment
# yoki-box runs from a worktree. The `.in` suffix (not `.template`) keeps
# core/config/manager.sh from generating it. This kit now embeds a host
# path — previously it did not, and was a plain spec.yaml (yoki-box keys the
# read-only dotfiles mounts off spec.yaml.in's presence, so converting it is
# what turns the mounts on below).
#
# Measured on the Codex base image 2026-08-15 (Ubuntu, user `agent`, home
# /home/agent, codex-cli 0.146.0): node, git, jq and rg are PRESENT; zsh,
# mise and fd are MISSING — the same three gaps as the Claude image, so the
# install list is identical.
#
# WHAT THIS KIT WRITES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
#
# 1. `jig apply --target codex --write` in startup (below) delivers ONLY the
#    Codex harness — ~/.codex/AGENTS.md, agents/*.toml, the ~/.agents/skills
#    mount and jig's `[mcp_servers.*]` block — and `jig codex register
#    --write` puts jig's PreToolUse guard into ~/.codex/hooks.json. Neither
#    touches ~/.claude, which does not exist in a Codex sandbox.
#
# 2. It does NOT overwrite ~/.codex/config.toml wholesale. sbx already
#    writes one at create time ("yolo mode": approval_policy = "never",
#    sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access") and that same file carries the
#    mcp-gateway server with its `Bearer proxy-managed` header — clobbering
#    it would silently cut MCP off. jig's block writer only ever touches the
#    marked block it owns (jig README §Milestone 3a), leaving sbx's
#    `sandbox_mode`/`approval_policy` lines and any `[mcp_servers.*]` table
#    outside it untouched; a server declared outside the block is reported,
#    not regenerated.
#
#    Net effect: inside this sandbox, Codex keeps running under sbx's own
#    yolo-mode permissions — jig's guard is the PreToolUse hook, not a
#    config-level permission table — on top of point 3 below.
#
# 3. It therefore leaves Codex more permissive than Claude in the same
#    posture even where the managed block DOES get written. Claude runs
#    `auto` — a classifier reviews every action — because a microVM stops
#    destruction but not disclosure (/run/sandbox/source is readable and the
#    Balanced policy allows 192 domains). Codex has no config-level
#    equivalent of `auto`: `on-request` leaves the decision to the model,
#    which a prompt injection simply declines to use, and `untrusted` asks
#    about everything. The nearest real equivalent is the `--approve-for-me`
#    launch flag (automatic review). Left unwired because sbx's own launch
#    arguments for the codex agent are not introspectable, so overriding
#    them here would be guesswork. Pass it yourself when it matters:
#        ycodex -- --approve-for-me
#    or layer a profile: put yoki.config.toml under $CODEX_HOME and run
#        ycodex -- -p yoki

permissions:
  network:
    allow:
      # apt on arm64 Ubuntu resolves to ports.ubuntu.com, not archive.
      - ports.ubuntu.com
      - archive.ubuntu.com
      - security.ubuntu.com
      # `curl https://mise.run | sh` redirects mise.run -> mise.jdx.dev, and
      # the installer pulls the binary from the GitHub release assets.
      - mise.run
      - mise.jdx.dev
      - github.com
      - objects.githubusercontent.com
      - release-assets.githubusercontent.com

setup:
  install:
    - command: apt-get update && apt-get install -y zsh fd-find
      description: The only base-image gaps besides mise (fd ships as fd-find on Ubuntu)
    - command: curl -fsSL https://mise.run | sh
      user: "1000"
      description: Install mise as the agent user so toolchains land in /home/agent
    # jig is a bun program; the base image has node 22 and npm but no bun.
    # Same three steps `jig box` generates (jig/src/app/box/kit.ts bunSteps):
    # npm's global bin is not on the bare PATH hooks get, hence the symlink.
    - command: npm install -g bun
      description: bun for the jig generator and guard (jig is a bun program)
    - command: ln -sf "$(npm prefix -g)/bin/bun" /usr/local/bin/bun
      description: Put bun on the bare PATH that Codex hooks get
    - command: cd {{DOTFILES_ROOT}}/domains/dev/llm/harness/jig && bun install --frozen-lockfile
      user: "1000"
      description: Resolve jig's dependencies once, before the first apply

  startup:
    # The guard policy jig's PreToolUse hook reads (JIG_POLICY_FILE, else
    # ~/.config/jig/policy/guard-rules.json) — the same link manager.sh's
    # link_jig_policy makes on the host. `$HOME`, not `{{HOME}}`: it must
    # resolve to the sandbox user's home at runtime, not the host's.
    - command:
        - sh
        - -c
        - mkdir -p "$HOME/.config/jig" && ln -sfn "{{DOTFILES_ROOT}}/domains/dev/llm/harness/policy" "$HOME/.config/jig/policy"
      user: "1000"
      description: Link jig's shared guard policy
    # jig resolves the checkout from its own path and honours CODEX_HOME, so
    # it delivers ~/.codex here exactly as `make link` does on the host (see
    # point 1 above). Two verbs: the harness, then the guard registration.
    - command:
        - sh
        - -c
        - bash {{DOTFILES_ROOT}}/domains/dev/bin/jig apply --target codex --write && bash {{DOTFILES_ROOT}}/domains/dev/bin/jig codex register --write
      user: "1000"
      description: Deliver ~/.codex with the jig generator and register jig's guard hook
    # Second layer: sbx writes `sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access"` at create
    # time (its "yolo mode", see point 2 above), which switches Codex's OWN
    # sandbox off inside the VM. The microVM is the boundary; Codex's
    # workspace-write sandbox is defense-in-depth under it, so turn it back
    # on. Only that one line is rewritten — the rest of config.toml (the
    # mcp-gateway table with its proxy-managed bearer) is left exactly as sbx
    # wrote it, for the reason point 2 gives. Shell network stays on inside
    # the VM because the VM's egress allowlist is what governs it; Codex's
    # own `network_access = false` would just block the allowlisted egress a
    # second time.
    - command:
        - sh
        - -c
        - |
          f="$HOME/.codex/config.toml"
          sed -i 's/^sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access"/sandbox_mode = "workspace-write"/' "$f"
          grep -q '^\[sandbox_workspace_write\]' "$f" || printf '\n[sandbox_workspace_write]\nnetwork_access = true\n' >> "$f"
          grep -n '^sandbox_mode' "$f"
      user: "1000"
      description: Turn Codex's own workspace-write sandbox back on inside the VM (sbx defaults it off)
    # No pack step, same as the claude kit: jig has no packs, so the one
    # apply above delivers the same Codex harness the host has.
